"""Finals rehearsal: plain-English answers with stable pre-existing question IDs.

The semi-final is frozen. New finals questions and the shared researched supplier
examples are added only to this finale. Proposed processes are labelled as such.
"""
from copy import deepcopy

ANSWERS = {
'q001': "Kairos helps people raise money for a clear need and shows donors what their money paid for. The organiser names the school, hospital or supplier and publishes a budget. Our intended live model pays that provider directly and gives each donor a record of their share. The prototype already runs the budget, records and updates; real money holding and payment still need the right partner arrangement.",
'q002': "There is no button for an organiser to withdraw donations into a personal account. Our payment code checks who is making the request, the named provider, the available balance and what remains in the budget. Those checks run on demo money today. Before live use, the institution holding the funds must also enforce the agreed payment restrictions.",
'q003': "The current receipt records a payment made inside the demonstration. It does not independently prove that a school received real money. For a live receipt, we would match our payment record to the payment partner’s confirmation and the relevant school bill. A document generated by Kairos alone is not enough proof of external payment.",
'q004': "Yes. A guest can give without opening an account, and the contribution appears anonymously in the cause’s records. A signed-in donor can receive personal updates in the app and, if they provide an email address, by email. Anonymous guest giving does not currently provide a personal account inbox or a private email receipt.",
'q005': "If a reference has expired, our proposed response is to pause the payment and ask for a replacement reference. We would confirm that it is for the same student, institution and bill, and check the amount and whether the bill has already been paid. A mismatch would go to a reviewer. The replacement-reference process is not built yet, so we should explain it as the next step.",
'q006': "A person should step in when a check is unclear, details disagree, someone challenges a decision or a payment’s outcome cannot be confirmed. The reviewer would ask for what is missing, check it through a trusted source and record the reason for the decision. We have not built a complete review queue and decision record yet.",
'q007': "Claude currently helps the organiser explain the need and turn that conversation into cause details and a budget. It does not independently certify a document, a person’s identity or a payment. We still need checks against the institution or an appropriate verification service. A convincing story or an uploaded document is supporting information, not a guarantee that the claim is true.",
'q008': "Draft attachments are available to their owner. Once attached to a published cause, they can be viewed publicly through the app. That means we need a separate place for private evidence, a way to remove personal details and clear permission before publication. Those additional protections must be completed before we use sensitive documents in real cases.",
'q009': "The current verification endpoint records a result and the type of check, rather than saving the NIN number. But that result is simulated today; it has not come from a live identity service. Also, a person could put identity details in an uploaded document. We therefore cannot turn this one design choice into a claim that no sensitive information is ever stored.",
'q010': "The current rule takes two percent of a donation, but total upkeep stops at five thousand naira for that cause. The cap is for the whole cause, not each donation or donor. The donor sees the charge and what will reach the cause before confirming. In our intended funding model, NGO support and grants are the preferred way to cover running costs.",
'q011': "We use the amount that reached the cause after any upkeep. For example, if your net contribution is one tenth of the cause’s funded amount, the displayed allocation for a payment from that pool is one tenth of that payment. This is a record of how pooled money was used, not a separate bank transfer from every donor. Later donations and rounding also need careful accounting.",
'q012': "No real bank payments have been matched to our records yet. The prototype maintains its own balances and payment records. For live use, we would compare those records with the payment partner’s confirmations using the payment reference, amount, destination and status. We also need to handle delays, failures and repeated messages without recording the same payment twice.",
'q013': "No. Donations and grants from NGOs are our intended main support, but none is secured in the material we have presented. The prototype applies the capped upkeep rule. We should not claim a funded operating budget, a working grant-based switch or proven running costs yet.",
'q014': "A complete real-money refund process is not implemented. We need to agree with the financial partner who can approve a refund, how it returns to the original donor and what happens to fees and already-spent money. Those rules must be shown before people give real funds. A proposed policy is not the same as a tested refund service.",
'q015': "Our own school-fee experience and a conversation with a past departmental president support the need we describe. He helps raise students’ fees and said personal trust does not always make the details easy to verify. We have also reported a positive response from departmental bodies. That is useful early feedback, but we have not presented a completed usability study or a real-money pilot.",
'q016': "We would start with a small number of school-fee cases. We would check whether the correct bills were settled, whether donors understood their receipts, how often a person had to resolve a problem and what it cost to handle each case. We would also ask organisers and donors where the process was confusing. These are proposed measures, not results we already have.",
'q017': "We need an agreed institution to hold and release the funds, connected payment and identity checks, private handling of evidence and a way to resolve mistakes, failed payments and refunds. We must also test that the records match the money and that a repeated request cannot pay twice. No signed custody partnership or live-money launch is being claimed.",
'q018': "The organiser's 26 November guidelines confirm a maximum of five minutes for the whole pitch, including the demonstration and funding plan. The current separate recording is two minutes forty seconds, so we need to rehearse the complete delivery against that total. The suggested section timings are guidance. Our page keeps an elapsed clock; it does not claim the retained script and unchanged recording already fit.",
'q019': "Suppose a school bill uses a student’s initials while the identity record uses the full name. We would pause the affected payment, explain the mismatch and ask for supporting details. An authorised reviewer would check with an independently contacted school office and record the decision. A real student should have a chance to correct a mismatch. This review process is proposed, not an existing feature.",
'q020': "We would not use the new reference until we had checked who and what it pays for. The student, institution, purpose and amount should agree with the cause, and the outstanding bill must be confirmed. If the replacement is for a different person or obligation, the payment stays paused while the issue is resolved. Automatic reference replacement is not implemented.",
'q021': "No. File upload and storage work, but automatic screening, removal of private details and a separate permission step for publication are still planned. Draft files are owner-only; attachments on published causes are public. We need to change that handling before presenting private-document review as an operational safeguard.",
'q022': "An uploaded document can contain sensitive details, so that phrase needs qualification. We do store account information, cause details and supporting files. Our intended approach is to keep identity checks with the right verification provider, keep private evidence separate and publish only material approved for that purpose. We have not finished the private-evidence, permission and redaction processes yet.",
'q023': "It supports the problem we are solving: even people who know a student can struggle to confirm an appeal and follow the money. The positive response is encouraging interest. It does not prove that people will use the product repeatedly, that the process is easy or that a real school bill has been paid through Kairos. We still need observed tests and a carefully prepared pilot.",
'q024': "The concern about checking appeals helped focus the idea on a named bill, a named provider and visible payment records. We do not have a documented product test showing a specific before-and-after improvement to claim. Our next step is to watch people use the flow, record their difficulties and show what we changed because of those findings.",
'q025': "I would show one uncertain case being resolved: a mismatch is explained, the affected payment is paused, evidence is checked and the reason for the decision is recorded. That would answer the judges’ concern about what happens when the ordinary flow breaks. It is a proposed next feature; we should not pretend the existing demo already shows it.",
'q026': "If you already know the student, the bill and the school’s payment channel, paying the school directly can be enough. Kairos helps when several people contribute and need a shared record of the bill, what remains unpaid and how their contributions were used. The value is making that shared process easier to check and follow.",
'q027': "A school-fee case gives us a specific student, institution, fee and payment reference to investigate. That is a manageable starting point for testing the process. It does not make every fee appeal genuine. We would still confirm the student’s connection to the bill, the outstanding amount and the correct destination before payment.",
'q028': "A typed business name is not enough. We would check that the provider exists, that the receiving details belong to it and that the bill is genuine. An unusual connection between the organiser and provider would need review. The prototype currently uses simulated provider checks, so it cannot be presented as having already ruled out fake providers.",
'q029': "A genuine provider could still cooperate with a dishonest organiser. Direct payment makes one kind of misuse harder, but it cannot remove every form of fraud. We would need independent bill checks, limits on the amount at risk and review of suspicious activity. We should not promise that registration guarantees honesty or that we can always recover money after it has been paid.",
'q030': "We would need to confirm that the beneficiary, or an appropriate representative, agrees to the fundraising and the information being published. A genuine bill does not give anyone permission to tell that person’s story publicly. The consent process is a requirement for live use and is not replaced by a convincing document.",
'q031': "Before real payouts, we would compare the institution, student and bill or reference across causes and check what remains owed. If the same bill has already been funded or paid, we must not pay it again. The prototype does not yet provide complete duplicate-bill detection across causes or live checks of whether an institution has received payment.",
'q032': "We would check the bill again before releasing money. If someone else has already settled it, the affected payment should pause and donors should be told. The remaining funds would follow the agreed refund or change-of-purpose rules. They should not quietly move to another need just because the organiser asks. Those live procedures are still to be built.",
'q033': "A change after donations could change what people agreed to support. Our proposed response is to pause the affected payment, check the reason and the replacement details, and obtain any permission required under the published rules. The review and change should be visible in the records. A complete budget-change and donor-consent process is not implemented yet.",
'q034': "We need rules for deadlines, partial funding and money that is no longer needed before collection begins. A school would need to confirm whether a partial payment is useful and accepted. If it is not, a refund or an agreed change may be needed. The prototype caps donations against its goal, but that alone is not a complete real-money closure and refund process.",
'q035': "In our intended arrangement, the remaining money would stay with the institution holding it. We would stop accepting instructions from the missing organiser, establish whether the bill still needs payment and follow the agreed closure or refund process. That depends on a real partner agreement and clear responsibilities, which are not secured yet.",
'q036': "It means checking that our records agree with the outside payment records. For each payment, we would match the reference, amount, recipient and result, then confirm the right bill was credited where needed. A disagreement stays unresolved until investigated. Our current app keeps an internal ledger; it is not yet connected to independent bank or school records.",
'q037': "We must first find out whether the original payment happened. Each instruction needs a saved, unique reference so a retry can find the same payment instead of creating a second one. If the result is unknown, we should check with the provider before trying again. We have not yet tested that failure-and-recovery process with a live payment partner.",
'q038': "Checking the balance twice is not enough if both requests arrive at the same moment. Before live use, the money for one instruction must be reserved in a single protected database operation, so the second request cannot spend it again. Each payment also needs a unique reference. The current checks are useful, but they do not establish that full guarantee.",
'q039': "We would recognise the payment or message reference and apply its effect once. Receiving the same confirmation twice must not reduce the balance twice or send two paid receipts. Late messages also need to be compared with the latest known result. This needs provider integration and testing; the current demonstration does not prove that handling is complete.",
'q040': "Not by itself. A provider may confirm processing before the receiving bank credits the money. We should report exactly what has been confirmed, then check that the correct student’s obligation was settled where necessary. Whether the student actually returned to school is a further outcome that would need its own evidence and appropriate permission.",
'q041': "Suppose you give ten thousand naira after upkeep and the cause receives one hundred thousand naira in total. Your contribution is ten percent of that pool. If forty thousand naira is paid from it, the displayed allocation to you is four thousand naira. It explains your share of a pooled payment; it is not a second bank transfer.",
'q042': "A later donation should never be described as paying for something that happened before it was given. We need to preserve which contributions were available for each payout and assign later donations to later spending. The prototype generates shares using the contributions recorded when it makes a payout. The full history for repeated payouts and later gifts still needs more testing before real money.",
'q043': "Not necessarily in the displayed messages, because the current app rounds individual shares to whole naira. That can leave a small difference when the messages are added together. For live accounting, we need to record the smallest supported currency unit and distribute any rounding remainder consistently. We should not claim perfect last-naira reconciliation from the current rounded messages.",
'q044': "The agreement with the institution holding the funds must explain what happens to the money and who can access the necessary records if Kairos stops operating or funds are frozen. We cannot promise a recovery route before that agreement exists. This is one of the questions we must settle before inviting people to donate real money.",
'q045': "Claude asks follow-up questions and helps turn the organiser’s answers into a story, named provider and budget. Our own code applies the payment and budget rules. Claude is not the institution holding the money and does not certify a bank transfer or identity check. The live Claude connection works today; the financial and identity checks shown in the demo are simulated.",
'q046': "People do not always arrive with a neatly written appeal. A conversation can ask about a missing bill, provider, amount or deadline and help them explain the situation. We still need to test whether it helps people more than a simple form. The model’s job is to organise the information, not make an unsupported story true.",
'q047': "The organiser should check the summary and budget before publishing, and a live payment should depend on checked provider and bill details. A model’s suggestion must not become proof of a price or permission to pay. Our code checks the output’s structure, but that does not establish that every generated detail is correct.",
'q048': "Text inside an uploaded document should be treated as material to inspect, not as instructions that can change the rules. Payment permission and approval should remain in controlled application code. We would need to test attempts to trick the model before claiming protection against them. The prototype does not establish that this whole class of attack has been solved.",
'q049': "The app keeps the organiser’s answer and offers a retry when the AI service fails. It should not silently replace Claude with a scripted reply and make that look like a real AI answer. Before wider use, we would also need to test interruptions, recovery and what support people need if the service stays unavailable.",
'q050': "AI may help identify details that need attention, but a model’s judgement should not be our only reason to publish someone’s private information. The intended process needs private evidence handling, removal of unnecessary details, clear permission and a person to resolve uncertain cases. That automatic publication-review process is not built in the current app.",
'q051': "No. A provider may perform one task well while Kairos still exposes a document, accepts the wrong payment instruction or gives someone too much access. We remain responsible for our own permissions, data handling and matching payment records. Each partner also needs to support our specific use case. A third-party connection is not a security guarantee for the whole product.",
'q052': "Next.js and TypeScript support the web application. MongoDB stores the cause, donation and payment records. Cloudinary stores uploaded files, and Claude helps with the interview. These services let us build and test the flow without building every piece of infrastructure ourselves. They do not replace the financial partner or the missing review and verification processes.",
'q053': "No. The public history makes activity visible, but it is still stored in our application database. We have not demonstrated a record that an administrator could never change. Live operation would need controlled access, records of changes and comparison with the financial partner’s records. We should describe the actual visibility we provide without claiming an unchangeable ledger.",
'q054': "The server must check requests even if someone avoids the normal screen. Our payout route checks sign-in, cause ownership, the vendor flag, the amount, balance and remaining budget. However, the identity and vendor checks behind some flags are simulated. Real verification and stronger payment controls are still needed before those flags can be trusted for live money.",
'q055': "A sensible starting point is the departmental bodies and community organisers who already help students raise fees. We have early interest, not a confirmed stream of causes or donors. We would first agree a small test with a willing institution and recruit people who understand what is being tested. We should not present those conversations as signed pilot commitments.",
'q056': "A school might benefit from clearer payment references and fewer unclear appeals, but we need to ask the relevant staff what would actually help them. We would need an agreed way to check bills and confirm payment without exposing unnecessary student information. We have not secured an institutional integration or permission simply by naming a school in the prototype.",
'q057': "A transfer can be convenient, especially when you know the person. Kairos adds a shared budget, a named destination and a record showing each donor’s share of what was paid. We still need to test whether donors find that worth using and what cost they will accept. We should explain the difference without pretending ordinary giving has no value.",
'q058': "Our focus is on connecting the stated need, a published budget, the provider payment and each donor’s own share. The donor can follow more than the total amount raised. That is the experience we are demonstrating. We still need a more complete comparison with other services and evidence that people prefer this approach in practice.",
'q059': "We are describing a design goal, not guaranteeing that fraud can never pay. Smaller exposure, direct provider payments and records can make some misuse harder to hide. People can still collude, lie or misuse genuine documents. We need independent checks and review, and we should be clear that the current prototype has not proved that every dishonest attempt becomes unprofitable.",
'q060': "We have not proved that it can. The cap limits what one cause contributes toward running costs, while verification and support still cost money. We need to measure those costs in a small pilot and compare them with reliable funding. Grants and NGO support are intended sources, but they are not secured. A working fee calculation is not proof of a sustainable operation.",
'q061': "If the cause has paid no upkeep yet and someone gives one hundred thousand naira, two thousand naira goes to upkeep and ninety-eight thousand reaches the cause. If total upkeep later reaches five thousand naira, no more upkeep is taken for that cause under the current rule. The example describes our platform upkeep; outside payment-provider charges still need to be agreed and disclosed.",
'q062': "We would have to match the scope of the service to funding we can actually rely on. That could mean a smaller pilot, fewer supported cases or a revised model that is explained before people give. We should not keep accepting real responsibilities on the assumption that a future grant will cover them. The current fallback fee is tentative, not a proven answer to every cost.",
'q063': "We would measure whether the correct bills were settled, donors understood the receipt, problems were resolved and people were willing to use the service again. We would also track privacy incidents, support effort and cost per case. Money raised alone can look impressive while a student’s actual bill remains unpaid. These are proposed measures, not completed results.",
'q064': "Agree a small school-fee test with willing organisers and an institution, while confirming the financial partner arrangement and the checks needed before any real funds. We should write down who reviews uncertain cases, what information stays private and what would make us stop the test. Then we can test the full process rather than add unrelated features.",
'q065': "We cannot give a reliable launch date before the external dependencies are agreed. The remaining work includes a suitable funds-holding arrangement, connected checks and payments, privacy controls and tested handling of mistakes and refunds. We can set a realistic plan once those responsibilities and access requirements are clear. Finishing presentation files does not mean the service is ready for real donations.",
'q066': "Hospitals can involve different billing processes, urgent decisions and particularly private information. Starting with a narrow school-fee case lets us test our basic process first. We would expand only after understanding the new verification and privacy needs. A hospital’s name in an example does not mean those additional processes have already been built.",
'q067': "The idea supports care for others with clear responsibility for how help is used. Technology can make it easier to check a need and follow a contribution, while people still have to make careful decisions and treat applicants with dignity. We see transparency as a way to support generosity without asking donors or applicants to ignore legitimate concerns.",
'q068': "No. The proposed service is for legitimate needs and people willing to follow its checks, whatever their faith. Participating in this event does not make religious membership a requirement for help. We would need to test that our process treats different people fairly and does not make access depend on who tells the most persuasive story.",
'q069': "For this presentation, Abigail is the team lead and product manager, coordinating priorities and the user journey. Ugochukwu is the software engineer and CTO, responsible for technical implementation and integration work. Those roles do not mean we already have a staffed review, compliance or customer-support operation. The responsibilities for a live pilot still need to be assigned clearly.",
'q070': "Introductions to an institution willing to test a school-fee case and a financial partner able to discuss the intended funds arrangement would be especially useful. Feedback on our review and privacy processes would also help. We would rather leave with a concrete next conversation and a realistic test than imply that an event connection is already a signed partnership.",
'q071': "A flag should lead to a check, not automatically label an honest person a fraudster. The applicant should understand the issue, have a chance to correct information and be able to ask a person to review the decision. We also need to test different language styles and circumstances. We have not completed that fairness testing or built a full appeal process.",
'q072': "A missing or failed check does not by itself prove dishonesty. We would need an agreed route for alternative evidence and human review that still protects the donor and beneficiary. Where we cannot support a case safely, we should explain that limit. We should not claim we can serve every genuine need before those alternatives are designed and tested.",
'q073': "We would test assisted use through trusted organisers, simpler wording and how the flow behaves on weak connections. The current prototype is a web application; it does not prove that someone without a smartphone or with limited English can complete it independently. Local-language and offline application support should be described as possibilities unless they have actually been built and tested.",
'q074': "They should only have to share the information needed for the cause and appropriate checks. Our intended process separates the public explanation from private evidence and requires agreement about what is published. The current public-attachment behaviour needs further work to support that safely. A person’s need for help should not be treated as permission to publish all their documents.",
'q075': "We would need a private reporting route so a concern can be investigated without publicly labelling someone dishonest. An authorised person should assess the evidence, pause affected activity where necessary and let the organiser respond. A suspicion and a proven finding are different things. The full reporting and appeal process is not implemented yet.",
'q076': "Checking that a provider exists does not prove that every amount it charges is justified. We would confirm the actual obligation and investigate unusual amounts through appropriate comparisons or an independent contact. A bill from a real institution can still need review. Automatic price checking and a complete overcharging process are not current features.",
'q077': "That is a possible later extension. We would first need a provider that permits this kind of contribution and clear records of the amount and charges before money reaches the Nigerian cause. Cleva is not connected to Kairos. Taking part in a competition does not establish provider access, permission to handle donations or a custody agreement.",
'q078': "We would stop public access, establish what information was exposed, support the affected person and follow the applicable response and notification process. We would also fix the underlying cause. We have not completed a production incident process. That is another reason private evidence and publication controls must be in place before we handle sensitive real cases.",
'q079': "We need a clear retention and deletion policy before live use. It should explain what is collected, who can see it, how long it stays and what happens when someone asks for deletion. Some payment records may need different treatment from an uploaded document. Deleting a public cause should not be described as automatically removing every stored or third-party copy.",
'q080': "We would agree stop conditions before the pilot, such as money records that do not match, an unresolved provider mismatch or private information being exposed. The affected activity should pause until the problem is understood. A small test should teach us whether the process is safe enough to continue, rather than become a reason to ignore a serious fault.",
}

FINALS = [
('feedback', 'What are the judges asking you to strengthen for the finals?', "They want us to explain who holds the funds, who handles uncertain decisions and how identity, organisations and beneficiaries are checked. They also want a focused example from the checks through to the donor’s receipt. We can demonstrate the application work already done while identifying the partnerships and processes still needed. Their email does not require us to finish every suggested feature before the finals."),
('custody-flow', 'Walk us through exactly where a real donation would go.', "In the proposed live arrangement, a donor pays through the agreed collection channel and the approved financial institution holds the money under the agreed rules. Kairos records the contribution and permits a payment request only for the checked provider and budget item. The financial partner executes the payment. We then match its result to our records and show the donor their share. The holding and settlement arrangement is not live yet."),
('custody-partner', 'Which institution is already holding money for Kairos?', "None is established in our current prototype or presentation materials. We have examples of banks, trustees and payment providers to approach, but we must confirm the exact service, permissions and agreement before collecting real funds. A payment gateway account alone would not establish the intended holding-and-release arrangement. We should state the dependency plainly rather than invent a signed partner."),
('partner-permission', 'What would you confirm with a financial partner before launch?', "We would explain the donation use case and confirm who holds the funds, who can approve a payment, which recipients are allowed and how fees, refunds and disputes work. We would also agree how we receive payment results and compare our records. The provider must support the particular arrangement we need. A general statement that it is licensed is not enough to answer those operational questions."),
('approval', 'Who requests, approves and actually sends the payment?', "The organiser would request a payment for the published budget item. Our rules and any required authorised review would check the bill, provider, amount and remaining funds. The institution holding or processing the money would execute an approved instruction under the agreed arrangement. We need clear separation of those responsibilities. The prototype currently demonstrates organiser permissions and checks, rather than a complete independent approval operation."),
('unknown-payment', 'What if the account is debited but the school says it received nothing?', "We would keep the payment under investigation and compare the reference and result with the provider and school. We should not assume failure and send it again while the outcome is unknown. The donor should see that it is pending or being checked, rather than a false success message. Once the outcome is established, the agreed retry, reversal or dispute process would apply. This is a live-operation requirement still to build."),
('reviewer', 'Who would the human reviewer be, and how would they work?', "We need to assign an authorised person with a clear procedure before a live pilot. They would see only the evidence needed, check it through an independently obtained institutional contact and record what they decided and why. The organiser should not approve their own exception. We have not hired or demonstrated a complete review team, so this is a responsibility to establish, not an operation we can already claim."),
('appeal', 'What if a legitimate applicant disagrees with the reviewer?', "They should receive an understandable reason and a way to supply corrected or additional information. A different authorised person should review a contested decision where possible, with any conflict of interest declared. The records should show what changed and why. We have not built that full appeal route or promised a response time; we need a workable procedure for the size of the first pilot."),
('override', 'Can a human reviewer simply override a failed check and release money?', "Human review should resolve the underlying issue, not switch off the payment rules. For example, the reviewer could confirm why the student’s initials differ from the school record and document that evidence. The provider, amount and budget limits must still be checked. A reviewer should not label an unresolved transfer as paid or direct the money to a new personal account. Those permissions and records need to be implemented."),
('four-checks', 'What is the difference between identity, organisation, beneficiary and provider checks?', "Identity asks who is organising the appeal. Organisation checks ask whether an institution or business exists and whether the person can act for it. Beneficiary checks ask whether the named person has the stated need and agrees to the appeal. Provider checks establish who should receive payment and whether the bill and receiving details belong together. Passing one check does not automatically answer the other three."),
('school-check', 'What would verification look like for one school-fee case?', "We would confirm the organiser’s identity and relationship to the student, the student’s agreement, the fee evidence and what is still owed. We would check the school and its official payment destination through a trusted route, then validate the reference and check for an already-paid or duplicate bill. After payment, we would confirm the correct bill was credited. The demo illustrates the intended flow; independent external checks are still simulated or unbuilt."),
('cac', 'How does CAC verification help, and what does it not prove?', "CAC records can help us compare a registered organisation’s name and registration details with the provider being named. We still need to check that the organiser is authorised, the receiving account belongs to the right institution and the bill is genuine. Registration does not prove honest conduct or a medical or education licence. A public institution may need its establishing law or relevant official register instead. Live CAC checking is not built in Kairos yet."),
('badge', 'What does a verified badge in the current demo actually mean?', "Some verification results are demonstration states. The current identity endpoint records a positive result without calling a live identity provider, and cause creation marks provider checks as passed for the demo. Those badges should not be offered as independent proof of a person or business. The live application logic around them works, but trusted results need a connected checking service and tested handling of failures."),
('all-third-parties', 'Your script says every third-party integration is simulated. Is that accurate?', "That sentence needs its scope explained. Claude, hosted application and database services, file storage and the email-sending path are actual services used by the prototype. Identity checks, licensed funds holding and external payment settlement are the intended integrations that are simulated or not built. We should name that distinction when asked rather than imply that the AI interview or hosting is also a mock."),
]

PLAIN_PARTNER_INTROS = {
'nin': "These are examples to investigate for checking identity details such as NIN or BVN. A lookup of an identity record, a comparison with a selfie and a check that a real person is present are different jobs. We must confirm access, permission and Nigeria coverage. Kairos’s current identity check is simulated.",
'face': "These providers offer different kinds of face comparison or checks that someone is present rather than a photograph. A face-check tool does not automatically give access to a Nigerian identity record. We would test the relevant phones and conditions before choosing a service. The demo does not establish a live face-verification connection.",
'kyb': "These are examples for checking registered organisations and their ownership details. We would compare the legal record with the named provider, its official contacts and separately checked account. Registration alone does not prove a bill is real or that the organiser can act for that institution. Live organisation checking is not built.",
'bank-name': "These are examples for checking a receiving account’s details. Looking up the name associated with an account helps us spot a mismatch, but we must still establish that it is the intended provider and that the bill belongs to this cause. A matching name does not establish the organiser’s authority.",
'escrow-custody': "These are institutions to approach about holding money and releasing it under agreed rules. Some explicitly describe escrow services; others are leads for a discussion about trust or charitable funds. We must confirm suitability, permissions and donation eligibility. None is being claimed as a signed Kairos custody partner.",
'escrow-tech': "These companies describe escrow-related technology. We would need to establish which institution actually holds the money, how it is protected and how releases, disagreements and refunds work. A website advertising escrow is not proof of an agreed or approved Kairos arrangement.",
'collections': "These are examples of services that could accept donations through supported payment channels. We would confirm that they permit the use case, what they charge and how they confirm a payment. Collecting a payment and holding donations under our intended release rules are separate responsibilities.",
'virtual-accounts': "A dedicated collection account or reference can help us identify which cause an incoming transfer belongs to. These providers are examples to investigate. An account number does not by itself establish who may hold the funds or which payments are allowed. The financial arrangement still needs to be agreed.",
'payouts': "These are examples of services that could send approved payments to a provider. We would check recipient details, payment references, result updates, delays and safe retries. The agreed institution holding the funds must also support the restrictions on where money may go. Kairos has not connected live payouts.",
'bills': "The school or hospital must support the actual bill-payment method. Remita is the relevant example for a Remita Retrieval Reference. Another provider’s bill API cannot automatically be treated as a way to validate that reference. We still need to confirm the beneficiary, amount, outstanding bill and institution’s acknowledgement.",
'cross-border': "These are possible options for later overseas contributions. We would need permission for the donation use case, supported donor countries and clear conversion charges and naira amounts before recording a contribution. An ordinary foreign-currency business account is not automatically a donation-platform integration. None of these is connected for this purpose today.",
'stablecoins': "These are optional future examples for different jobs around digital currencies, conversion or wallet technology. Their services are not interchangeable, and this is not required for our first school-fee case. We would need to confirm supported assets, countries, permissions and who holds the funds. No stablecoin funding or custody integration is claimed.",
'aml': "These services can help check restricted-person lists or other financial-risk information. Those checks are different from proving that a student owes school fees. We would follow the agreed financial partner’s requirements and have a person review uncertain results. This provider list is research, not an operating Kairos compliance service.",
'fraud': "These are examples of tools that look for unusual activity, suspicious devices or payment patterns. A warning is a reason to investigate, not proof that a particular appeal is false. We would need to test the tool and handle mistakes fairly. No tool makes the whole product automatically secure.",
'evidence': "Some examples below are verification businesses; public bodies are authoritative information sources rather than proposed commercial partners. We would still contact the school’s fees office or the hospital’s finance office through an independently obtained route to confirm the actual bill. A registered organisation alone does not prove this beneficiary’s need.",
'document-checks': "Some tools read text and amounts from invoices; others look for possible alterations. These are different tasks. Neither automatically proves that a Nigerian school or hospital issued the document or is still owed money. We would check with the issuer where needed and keep a person involved when the evidence is uncertain.",
'reconciliation': "These examples could help compare our records with payment-provider records. The essential job is to match the amount, recipient, reference and outcome, including refunds or reversals. General record-matching tools do not become Nigerian funds-holding partners. Kairos remains responsible for its budget rules and donor-share calculations.",
'ai': "Claude is the AI service currently used for the interview and cause details. The other names are alternatives to investigate, not integrations already in Kairos. We would compare answer quality, cost, reliability and data handling. An AI interview is separate from independently checking identity, bills or payments.",
'storage': "Cloudinary currently stores supporting files. The other names are storage alternatives. We would still have to decide who can see a file, what may be published and how long information is kept. Today draft files are owner-only and published attachments can be viewed publicly through Kairos. Storage does not establish document authenticity.",
'email': "These are examples of companies that deliver application emails. Kairos uses Nodemailer, which is a sending library rather than a delivery company. We have not named one of these as our current delivery provider here. Email is optional, and sending a receipt does not by itself prove a school received payment.",
'messaging': "These are possible services for SMS, one-time codes or WhatsApp updates. They are optional future channels, not existing Kairos messaging integrations. Sharing a normal cause link on WhatsApp is different from connecting its business-messaging service. Delivery support and permission to send the message would need to be checked.",
'hosting': "Vercel and MongoDB Atlas are already used for the application and database. The other examples are alternatives, not extra financial partners. Hosting can help us run the product, but we still have to control access, protect information, keep backups and check payment records. A cloud account does not solve those jobs automatically.",
}

def build_questions(base):
    from cleva_partner_directory import partner_questions
    from faith_and_tech_compliance_questions import compliance_questions
    existing=deepcopy(base)
    assert {q['id'] for q in existing}==set(ANSWERS)
    for q in existing:q['answer']=ANSWERS[q['id']]
    finals=[dict(id='ff-'+key,category='Finals feedback: rehearse first',question=question,answer=answer,priority=True) for key,question,answer in FINALS]
    partners=deepcopy(partner_questions())
    for q in partners:
        service=q['id'][3:];intro=PLAIN_PARTNER_INTROS[service]
        q.update(id='fp-'+service,category='Third parties: examples for each role',priority=False,partner_intro=intro,
                 answer=intro+'\n\n'+'\n'.join(p['name']+': '+p['role']+'.' for p in q['partners']))
    questions=finals+compliance_questions()+partners+existing
    for i,q in enumerate(questions,1):q['display_number']=i
    assert len({q['id'] for q in questions})==len(questions)
    assert len({q['question'] for q in questions})==len(questions)
    categories=list(dict.fromkeys(q['category'] for q in questions))
    groups=[(category,[(q['question'],q['answer'],q['priority']) for q in questions if q['category']==category]) for category in categories]
    return groups,questions
